@* Principle 7 – Content does not cause seizures and physical reactions *@

<div class="callout">
    <p><strong>This page contains no flashing content.</strong> The examples below describe flashing; they don't show it.</p>
</div>

<h2>The three-flashes rule</h2>
<p>
    Content must not flash more than <strong>three times in any one second</strong>, unless the flashing area is
    very small or below the brightness thresholds WCAG defines. Saturated red flashes are especially dangerous.
    The safest choice is simply not to flash. Watch out for:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>strobe effects in videos, games, and animated GIFs;</li>
    <li>rapidly alternating light and dark backgrounds or “alert” effects;</li>
    <li>fast blinking text or banners.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    The free <a href="https://trace.umd.edu/peat/">Photosensitive Epilepsy Analysis Tool (PEAT)</a> can check video for dangerous flashes.
</p>

<h2>Warn before flashing content</h2>
<p>
    If content might trigger a reaction, warn users <em>before</em> it starts and offer an alternative.
    Nothing should start until the user chooses.
</p>
<div class="card" id="flash-warning">
    <h3>Content warning</h3>
    <p>The next video clip of a concert contains strobe lighting and flashing lights.</p>
    <div class="button-row">
        <button type="button" id="flash-alt">Read a text description instead</button>
        <button type="button" class="button-secondary" id="flash-continue">Continue to the video</button>
    </div>
    <div id="flash-result" tabindex="-1" hidden></div>
</div>

<h2>Let users turn animation off</h2>
<p>
    Animation such as parallax scrolling, zooming, and large moving elements can cause dizziness and nausea for
    people with vestibular disorders. Respect the operating system's <em>reduce motion</em> setting, and offer a
    control on the page too.
</p>
<p id="motion-os-status">Checking your operating system's motion setting…</p>

<div class="motion-stage" id="motion-stage">
    <div class="motion-ball" aria-hidden="true"></div>
</div>
<div class="button-row">
    <button type="button" class="button-secondary" id="motion-pause">Pause animation</button>
</div>
<p>
    You can also stop <em>all</em> animation on this site with the <strong>Reduce motion</strong> button in the
    page footer. Your choice is remembered on this device.
</p>

<p>This is the CSS that makes it work:</p>
<code-sample label="Respecting reduced motion in CSS">
    /* Honor the operating system setting */
    @@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        *, *::before, *::after {
            animation-duration: 0.01ms !important;
            animation-iteration-count: 1 !important;
            transition-duration: 0.01ms !important;
        }
    }
</code-sample>

<p>
    To turn on reduced motion: on Windows, go to <em>Settings → Accessibility → Visual effects → Animation effects</em>.
    On macOS, go to <em>System Settings → Accessibility → Display → Reduce motion</em>.
</p>

@section Scripts {
    <script src="~/js/seizures.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>
}
